---
<Guide title="Writing the 5 positive and 3 negative test cases for a ChatGPT plugin" seoTitle="ChatGPT Plugin Review Test Cases (5 + 3)" description="Remote MCP plugins need exactly 5 positive and 3 negative test cases. Required fields per OpenAI's schema, and how to write cases reviewers can verify." summary="a remote MCP plugin needs exactly five positive and three negative test cases plus release notes. Positive cases need description, user_prompt and tools_triggered; negative cases need description and user_prompt. Write prompts a reviewer can run on your demo account and get the result you describe." sources={['submissionErrors', 'submissionSchema', 'appReview']}>
  <p class="lede">The submission reference says a remote MCP plugin needs “exactly five positive test cases, three negative test cases, and release notes”. The review page lists failed test cases as a common rejection reason. Here is how to get them right.</p>

  <h2>Fields per case</h2>
  <p>OpenAI publishes a JSON schema for the submission import file, <a href="https://developers.openai.com/apps-sdk/schemas/chatgpt-app-submission.v1.json" rel="noopener"><code>chatgpt-app-submission.v1.json</code></a>. (The file name still says “ChatGPT app submission”.) It defines:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th></th><th>Required</th><th>Optional</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Positive case</td><td><code>description</code> (≤ 4,000 chars), <code>user_prompt</code>, <code>tools_triggered</code></td><td><code>expected_output</code>, <code>expected_output_url</code>, <code>file_attachment_urls</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Negative case</td><td><code>description</code>, <code>user_prompt</code></td><td><code>tools_triggered</code>, <code>expected_output</code>, <code>expected_output_url</code>, <code>file_attachment_urls</code></td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>The schema sets <code>minItems</code> of 5 and 3; the submission page says exactly 5 and 3. Aim for exactly. The schema also expects, per tool, the three annotation booleans and three justification strings — while the guidelines say justifications are no longer required. Our <a href={u('/tools/test-case-linter/')}>test-case linter</a> keeps the file schema-valid either way.</p>

  <h2>Positive cases: make them verifiable</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Use the demo account’s real sample data so the reviewer gets the answer you describe.</li>
    <li>Write <code>expected_output</code> precisely: what list, which fields, roughly how many items.</li>
    <li>Cover your main tools; don’t spend all five on one.</li>
    <li>Be careful with state-changing tools. A reviewer will actually run the prompt. Make the action safe to repeat on the demo account, and say what will happen.</li>
    <li>The review page warns that output should not offer “extraneous information that is irrelevant to the request, including personal identifiers”.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Negative cases: show restraint</h2>
  <p>A negative case is a prompt where your plugin should <em>not</em> be used, or should decline. Good ones are near-misses: related to your domain but outside what the plugin does, or general-knowledge questions the model can answer itself.</p>

  <h2>Common mistakes the linter catches</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Four or six positive cases instead of five.</li>
    <li><code>tools_triggered</code> naming a tool that isn’t in <code>tools/list</code> (renamed tools are the usual cause).</li>
    <li>Vague prompts such as “test” or “help me”.</li>
    <li><code>@mentions</code> in prompts.</li>
    <li>Tools without explicit <code>readOnlyHint</code>, <code>openWorldHint</code> and <code>destructiveHint</code> (see <a href={u('/guides/mcp-tool-annotations/')}>MCP tool annotations explained</a>).</li>
  </ul>
</Guide>
